.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt AFTERBOOT 8
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm afterboot
.Nd building, booting, and checking the operator image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This page is for the operator of an ABSD system: how to get the operator
image, boot it, check it, and look after it, and what it cannot do yet.
It describes the qualified system (see
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Sx QUALIFICATION ) :
a served
.Ql net fsd
boot under QEMU, whose file server holds the disk and whose SSH server is
the only way in.
.Ss Build the image
The operator image is built on a Linux host from the ABSD platform
repository and a checkout of the ABSD kernel at the commit the platform's
.Pa scripts/check-all.sh
names:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f operator-key
$ ABSD_KERNEL_RUN=/path/to/kernel-checkout \e
    scripts/build-operator-image.sh \e
    --authorized-keys operator-key.pub [--etc DIR] OUT
.Ed
.Pp
It builds the platform, then writes
.Pa OUT/absd-operator.iso
(GRUB, the kernel, init, the file server, and the SSH server),
.Pa OUT/absd-operator.img
(a 64 MiB disk), and
.Pa OUT/MANIFEST
(the two commits and the SHA-256 of every file installed).
The disk holds every command under
.Pa /bin ,
this manual under
.Pa /share/man ,
.Pa /etc/network.conf
and
.Pa /etc/sshd.conf
(the files in
.Ar DIR
in place of the defaults), and the keys under
.Pa /ssh/authorized_keys ,
which must be
.Ql ssh-ed25519
lines without options.
Files are written by ABSD itself, in importer boots under QEMU, and the host
reads every one back before the build succeeds.
The first boot adds
.Pa /home/operator ,
.Pa /tmp ,
.Pa /var/log ,
and the host key.
.Ss Boot
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ scripts/boot-operator.sh OUT 2222
.Ed
.Pp
This runs QEMU in the foreground with RDRAND
.Pq Ql -cpu max,vendor=GenuineIntel,+rdrand ,
the ISO, the disk as the primary IDE master
.Pq Xr ata 4 ,
one e1000 card on QEMU's user network with 127.0.0.1:2222 forwarded to
the address and port the disk's
.Pa /etc
files name, and the serial console in
.Pa OUT/console.log .
It returns when the machine halts
.Pq status 0
or the boot fails (QEMU's status, 7 for a failed boot).
A
.Xr reboot 8
boots the same image again within the same run.
Without RDRAND the SSH server refuses to start
.Pq Ql sshd: entropy: ...
and the boot fails.
.Ss Check the host key
The SSH server prints its host key's fingerprint on the console at every
start, in plain text, in the form
.Ql ssh-keygen -l
uses, and the public key itself:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ grep '^sshd: host key' OUT/console.log
sshd: host key 256 SHA256:3n0X...Q (ED25519)
sshd: host key ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz...
.Ed
.Pp
Compare the
.Ql SHA256:
value with the one
.Nm ssh
shows on the first connection
.Pq Dq ED25519 key fingerprint is SHA256:...
before accepting it, or put the public key in a known_hosts file yourself
and keep strict checking on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo "[127.0.0.1]:2222 ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz..." > known_hosts
$ ssh -p 2222 -i operator-key -o UserKnownHostsFile=known_hosts \e
    -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes operator@127.0.0.1
absd$
.Ed
.Pp
The same lines are in
.Pa /var/log/sshd .
The file server creates the key at the first boot
.Pq Ql FSD HOST KEY GENERATED
and loads it after
.Pq Ql FSD HOST KEY PRESENT .
It lives at
.Pa /ssh/host_ed25519_seed
on the disk; no session can read, list, or reach
.Pa /ssh ,
but anyone holding the disk image can read the key.
.Pp
Only public keys are accepted.
A line of
.Pa /ssh/authorized_keys
with options or another key type makes the server refuse the whole file.
There are no passwords and no accounts; the user name is logged and
otherwise ignored.
Every key in the file is the same single operator.
.Ss Look around
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ uname -a
absd$ release
absd$ date
absd$ uptime
absd$ ls /
absd$ df
absd$ man intro
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql ls /
shows the session's view:
.Pa bin/ ,
.Pa etc/ ,
.Pa home/ ,
.Pa share/ ,
.Pa tmp/ ,
.Pa var/ .
.Nm df
prints the filesystem's size, the blocks in use and free, the commit
sequence on the disk, and this session's mounts with their access; every
session shares one filesystem and there are no quotas.
.Nm release
names the release being served: the commands in
.Pa /bin
and the manual in
.Pa /share
belong to it, and everything else to this machine
.Pq Xr release 1 .
.Ss Where things live
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/session-N"
.It Pa /home/operator
Read-write.
Kept across sessions and boots; shared by every session.
Put your files here.
.It Pa /tmp/session-N
Read-write, this session only: no other session can see it.
It is removed when the session ends.
.It Pa /bin
The commands.
Read-only.
.It Pa /etc
The configuration,
.Xr network.conf 5
and
.Xr sshd.conf 5 .
Read-only.
.It Pa /share
This manual
.Pq Pa /share/man .
Read-only.
.It Pa /var/log
The logs and retained owner network sample; see
.Xr net-status 1 .
Read-only.
.It Pa /ssh
The host key and authorized keys.
Not visible to any session.
.El
.Pp
Read-only means read-only for sessions: those files are placed when the
image is built or reconfigured, and no session changes them.
.Ss Configuration
.Pa /etc/network.conf
sets the address, netmask, and gateway;
.Pa /etc/sshd.conf
sets the port, the number of connections, the login grace time, the idle
timeout, and the authentication tries.
The SSH server reads both at start and prints what it uses:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sshd: network 10.0.2.15/24 gateway 10.0.2.2 (/etc/network.conf)
sshd: port 22, at most 4 connections, login grace 30 s, idle
timeout 1800 s, 6 authentication tries (/etc/sshd.conf)
.Ed
.Pp
A missing file means the defaults, and the line says so.
A malformed file is refused: the console says which file, which line, and
why, the SSH server does not start, and the boot fails.
Sessions cannot write
.Pa /etc ,
so a session can never lock the operator out.
To change or fix a file, halt the machine and replace the file on the
image, then boot again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ABSD_KERNEL_RUN=/path/to/kernel-checkout \e
    scripts/build-operator-image.sh --reconfigure --etc DIR OUT
.Ed
.Pp
Each file of
.Ar DIR
replaces its namesake whole, in one commit; everything else on the disk
is kept.
.Fl -authorized-keys Ar KEYS
replaces the keys the same way.
.Ss Logs
The file server writes
.Pa /var/log/fsd
and the SSH server
.Pa /var/log/sshd ,
each itself; there is no log daemon.
Every line begins with the time, UTC, from the real-time clock:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ tail -n 3 /var/log/sshd
2026-09-27T05:34:12Z sshd: conn 7: authenticated
.Ed
.Pp
.Pa /var/log/fsd
begins each boot with
.Ql fsd: started: boot ID ,
the identity
.Xr uptime 1
prints, and then records how the previous generation ended
.Pq Ql FSD RECOVERY ;
.Xr health 1
reads that line for this boot.
When a log would grow past 64 KiB it is renamed to
.Pa NAME.0 ,
replacing the older one, and started again: each log keeps at most
128 KiB, the newest lines.
If the filesystem refuses a write (it is full), the line is lost and the
next line written says how many were.
The lines still go to the console as well.
Init's lines, and every line before the file server starts, are on the
console only: init has no file server channel.
Per-command lines of the file server
.Pq Ql FSD DERIVE
are on the console only.
There is no
.Ql tail -f .
.Ss Copying files
There is no
.Nm scp
or
.Nm sftp :
the SSH server runs no subsystems.
Use a command with a redirection:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 'cat > /home/operator/f' < f
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 'cat >> /home/operator/f' < more
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 cat /home/operator/f > f
.Ed
.Pp
Under QEMU (TCG) a 1 MiB file took 45 to 60 s up and about 17 s down.
Both directions are byte-exact for any file, binary included, as long as
no terminal is requested (no
.Fl t ;
a terminal changes line endings and takes control characters as keys).
A command with a
.Ql | ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql > ,
.Ql \&; ,
.Ql & ,
or
.Ql \&"
is run by
.Xr absd-sh 1
.Po
.Ql absd-sh -c
.Pc ;
any other command runs directly.
Paths are absolute, or relative to
.Pa / ,
which only reads.
The upload is committed when
.Nm cat
ends; check it with
.Ql ssh host sha256sum FILE .
An upload the client breaks off leaves the file cut short (the old
contents are gone once
.Ql >
opens it).
Nothing of the upload keeps running after the client goes: the shell ends
.Nm cat
and exits.
Nothing is copied that the session could not write itself.
.Ss What is persistent
A file written under
.Pa /home/operator
is committed to the disk when it is closed, when
.Nm sync
is run, when the session ends, and at most about 200 ms after a write that
was not otherwise committed.
.Nm sync
returns once everything the file server has accepted, from every
session, is on the disk.
After a commit the file survives the machine stopping abruptly; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.Ss Time
.Xr date 1
reads the real-time clock: UTC, whole seconds, never set or corrected by
ABSD.
.Xr uptime 1
counts kernel ticks since boot and names the boot.
A file records one time, its modification time
.Pq Xr filesystem 7 ;
stat shows it as UTC ISO time (or unknown), and ls -l shows UTC to the
minute (or -). No command sets it.
.Ss Limits
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Four accepted SSH connections at once, the default and the most
.Xr sshd.conf 5
admits (what the kernel's 48 pipes serve), counting unauthenticated ones.
A further completed TCP connection is promptly closed without an SSH
session slot, file-server binding, or banner. The console records
.Ql SSH GLOBAL REFUSED ... limit=4 .
Once a session releases its slot, an authenticated connection can use it.
.It
At most two accepted unauthenticated connections from one IPv4 source
while global slots remain. A further one is closed with
.Ql SSH PREAUTH REFUSED ... reason=source-limit .
An authenticated connection still counts toward the global four.
This source limit applies after TCP accept, not to the SYN/listener backlog.
.It
Sixteen processes in the whole system, including init, the file server,
and the SSH server.
.It
Sixty-four handles per process, 32 live cells, and 48 live pipes in the
whole system. The default, non-served boot suite exercises full-table
refusal, release, and reuse for process, handle, cell, and pipe tables;
the served-boot max-four case exercises its SSH limit separately.
Printed limits and occupancy counts alone do not show that every table
was exhausted in a served boot.
.It
Commands cannot start programs; only a session's shell can
.Pq Xr absd-sh 1 .
When a session ends, its shell ends the commands it started.
If the SSH server has to kill the shell instead
.Pq Ql SSH SESSION END ... forced=true ,
those commands can outlive the session, and a
.Xr shutdown 8
or
.Xr reboot 8
then stops the machine rather than halting or restarting it.
.It
Eleven file server channels: one control channel and ten client slots
shared by sessions and the commands they run; see
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
.It
One CPU for served/user execution, one network card, one disk.
CPU 1 is reserved for separate kernel-only fixtures; served boot with
that AP option is refused.
.El
.Ss How the boot ends
The boot runs until the operator asks for
.Xr shutdown 8
or
.Xr reboot 8 ,
from
.Ql ssh host shutdown
or from an interactive shell; every session is told and ends, and the file
server commits everything before the machine halts or restarts.
Stopping QEMU otherwise is an abrupt stop: anything not yet committed may
be lost, and the next boot finds the last commit.
.Ss What you cannot do yet
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Change the configuration from a session, or without a reboot.
.It
Follow a log as it grows, or send logs elsewhere.
.It
See or stop processes of other sessions
.Pf ( Xr ps 1
lists only your own session).
.It
Add users; there is one operator.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On the console
.Pq Pa OUT/console.log :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql sshd: /etc/FILE: line N: REASON; fix the file ...; sshd is not running
a malformed configuration file; the boot fails.
Fix it with
.Fl -reconfigure .
.It Ql FSD FAILED mount: ...
the file server refused the disk (a damaged or foreign filesystem).
Nothing was changed; the boot fails.
There is no repair tool; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.El
.Pp
The SSH server's other lines reach the console inside init's hex-framed
output records
.Pq Ql EXTERNAL OUTPUT CHUNK ... data=HEX ,
the qualification format; read them in
.Pa /var/log/sshd
instead.
.Sh SEE ALSO
